    {
      "id": "rn_NPzyvfByCy3bwH6f::L1::Q8",
      "moduleId": "rn_NPzyvfByCy3bwH6f::L1",
      "stem": "Explain why mitochondria and ribosomes are described with DIFFERENT jobs even though both are tiny subcellular structures.",
      "misconceptionClass": "division of labour",
      "kind": "explain-why",
      "modelAnswer": "Each structure is built for its task: mitochondria provide the internal surface and enzymes for aerobic respiration, while ribosomes assemble amino acids into proteins, so one is the power plant and the other the assembly bench rather than interchangeable parts.",
      "keywords": [
        "mitochondria",
        "ribosomes",
        "proteins",
        "interchangeable"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_NPzyvfByCy3bwH6f::L1::Q9",
      "moduleId": "rn_NPzyvfByCy3bwH6f::L1",
      "stem": "A cell sample shows dense mitochondria but very few ribosomes. Which activity would this cell favour, and what does the scarcity of ribosomes suggest?",
      "misconceptionClass": "inventory application",
      "kind": "application",
      "modelAnswer": "Dense mitochondria point to high energy demand through aerobic respiration; few ribosomes suggest little protein manufacture, so the cell imports rather than builds most of its proteins.",
      "keywords": [
        "mitochondria",
        "ribosomes",
        "proteins",
        "respiration"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
